  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nTemperatures have continued to warm into the low to mid 40s with \nonly mid and high clouds around the area and plenty of opportunities\nfor sunshine. A couple CAMs then showing weak signal for patchy \nfog development late tonight, though with dry airmass in place and\nsufficient mixing, think fog coverage, if any, will be held to a \nminimum. Lows reading mainly in the low to mid 30s should mitigate\nany concerns for slippery patches on roads, as was the case this \nmorning.\n\nDry cold front passes through the Great Lakes late tonight into \nSunday morning as surface low tracks through Ontario, shifting\nwinds to the northwest across the forecast area. Pressure gradient\ntightens up along the back edge of the departing low during this\ntime, increasing wind gusts into the 25 to 35 mph range Sunday\nafternoon as deep daytime mixing taps into near 40 knot winds at \n850 mb. Highest gusts will be over the Northwoods.\n\nHighs flirt with 60 degrees across most of northeast Wisconsin\ninto the middle of next week as warming ridge builds over the\nPlains. Next frontal passage then looks to be Tuesday into\nWednesday, during which the blend introduces 15 to 20% PoPs, \nmainly over the lake effect belts. Colder shot of air arrives \nduring this time as 850 mb temps fall to -8 to -10C, hinting at\nthe potential for flurries mixing in with light rain at times,\nespecially as we enter the season where any low stratus could\nproduce a few flakes. Otherwise, respectable signal (50 to 60%) \nexists for below normal precipitation into the middle of the \nmonth.",
